ai: To create a rotation matrix for a 2D Cartesian coordinate system around a specific point ((h, k)), you can follow these steps:


Translate the point of rotation to the origin: This involves subtracting the coordinates of the point ((h, k)) from the coordinates of the point you want to rotate.


Apply the rotation: Use the standard rotation matrix for an angle (\theta):
[
R(\theta) = \begin{bmatrix}
\cos(\theta) &amp; -\sin(\theta) \
\sin(\theta) &amp; \cos(\theta)
\end{bmatrix}
]


Translate back: After rotating, you need to translate the point back to its original position.


The complete transformation can be expressed as follows:


Translate the point ((x, y)) to the origin:
[
x’ = x - h \
y’ = y - k
]


Rotate the point:
[
\begin{bmatrix}
x” \
y”
\end{bmatrix}
= R(\theta) \cdot \begin{bmatrix}
x’ \
y’
\end{bmatrix}
= \begin{bmatrix}
\cos(\theta) &amp; -\sin(\theta) \
\sin(\theta) &amp; \cos(\theta)
\end{bmatrix} \cdot \begin{bmatrix}
x - h \
y - k
\end{bmatrix}
]


Translate back:
[
x_{\text{final}} = x” + h \
y_{\text{final}} = y” + k
]


Putting it all together, the final coordinates after rotation around the point ((h, k)) are:
[
\begin{bmatrix}
x_{\text{final}} \
y_{\text{final}}
\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos(\theta) &amp; -\sin(\theta) \
\sin(\theta) &amp; \cos(\theta)
\end{bmatrix} \cdot \begin{bmatrix}
x - h \
y - k
\end{bmatrix} + \begin{bmatrix}
h \
k
\end{bmatrix}
]
This gives you the new coordinates ((x_{\text{final}}, y_{\text{final}})) after rotating the point ((x, y)) around the point ((h, k)) by an angle (\theta).
